soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: crust's contract page in one header, and the version it must have

The words this driver shares with crust were constants in the driver,
and only the keep path looked at the version, as "at least 4" with no
upper bound. A freshly loaded crust was driven at the fixed offsets
whatever its page said; the log named no variant on that path, and on
the keep path only bits 24-26 of the build word.

linux/soc/sunxi/h713-crust-contract.h is contract version 5: the page
layout with its lifetime classes and one writer per word, the build word
and the second build word, the standby state with the causes of crust's
clean reset, its request, budget and confirmation words with the order
Linux writes them in (the last confirmation cleared first), TF-A's word,
the record a loader leaves before it restarts crust (P) with its
checksum, an encoder and a decoder for each of these words, and the
acceptance rule all three sides use:

  magic == "H713" && version >= MIN && compat_eff <= MAX,
  compat_eff = version >= 5 ? COMPAT[7:0] : version

with COMPAT the oldest reader a page still suits. The header needs
nothing from the kernel, so a host test builds the same file.

The driver reads the HDMI service from contract 4 to 5 and checks the
rule on the keep path (a crust it cannot read is loaded over) and after
the handshake of a crust it loaded (crust writes magic and version
before the notify; a page it cannot read leaves the firmware unused, no
HDMI command goes to offsets that may mean something else). Both paths
then log one line with the version, the build word decoded (bits 24-31)
and the second build word (DRAM clock, board, R_WDOG interval); a test
build is a warning. A kept crust whose notify an earlier probe took is
named by the version on its page instead of a placeholder.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
